        "1": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 31,
                "quote": "match library.Loans |> Map.tryFind copy.Id with | Some loan -> $\"on loan until {Views.date loan.DueDate}\" | None -> \"on shelf\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "Branch validation (Catalog.findBranch), copies at a branch (Catalog.copiesAt) and hold queues (Holds.queueFor) all come from Tier 2. The Tier 3 report also derives the copy status itself (on loan or on shelf) from the Loans map. That is a small status derivation outside Tier 1/2. It looks like projection and does not contradict Tier 2, so it scores 1. I could only judge the diff, so I could not check whether Tier 2 already has an equivalent status function.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "All new file output goes through the existing port: `ports.Files.WriteLines path (...)` in exportInventory, exportHolds and exportMembers. Data is read through `ports.Store.Load()`. No clock, I/O or console call is added in Tier 1/2, Tier 3 or the Tier 4 presentation code. The CLI only calls Tier 3 use cases and renders views. The pre-existing direct `File.WriteAllLines` in exportOverdue is not part of this change.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The new facts are the three CSV headers and the status strings. Each is written once. Member type names come from the existing `Views.memberTypeName`, and dates come from `Views.date`. Hold order comes from `Holds.queueFor`. No limit, rate or date rule is copied. The only restated item is the `member,type,name` header, and the importer for it is not in the diff.",
            "score": 2
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 35,
                "quote": "match Holds.queueFor copy.Book library with | [] -> \"on shelf\" | queue -> $\"on shelf ({queue.Length} holds waiting)\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "Import legality is decided in Tier 2: `Catalog.addCopy` and `Catalog.findBranch` are called, and the rows are folded all-or-nothing through `commit`. The Tier 3 code only parses the CSV and numbers the lines. The held-copy status is a new rule that Tier 3 decides itself: when a shelved copy counts as held, and the hold count taken from the queue length. It uses `Holds.queueFor` and does not contradict Tier 2, but the rule sits in Tier 3, so it scores 1.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "importCopies reads the file through `ports.Files.ReadLines path` and persists through the existing `commit`. `cannot read` is returned as a value when `ReadLines` returns None. The CLI only dispatches to the use case and renders the view. No effect is added below Tier 4 and no port is bypassed.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The hold count comes from `Holds.queueFor`, the single source for queues. The branch rule comes from `Catalog.findBranch` and the copy rules from `Catalog.addCopy`. Error text comes from `Views.describeError`. The header-skip and blank-line filter copies the importMembers pattern, but it is a parsing convention and not a domain fact, and the two agree. There are no duplicated limits or literals that could drift.",
            "score": 2
          }
